\section*{Positive lower density of arbitrarily large normalized prime gaps}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 026; 2026-09-25), the asserted result is that each fixed threshold $C\log p$ is exceeded by a proportion of consecutive prime gaps bounded away from zero, which also gives a positive lower proportion of increases in $p_n/n$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 026; 2026-09-25), a square of signed divisor sums gives appreciable weight to a prime in one short interval and little weight to a prime in the adjacent interval. Two second-moment bounds convert weighted mass to ordinary starts; bounded multiplicity then counts distinct consecutive gaps.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 026; 2026-09-25).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 026; 2026-09-25), the documented Lean scope is positive lower density of increases in the sequence $p_n/n$; the full large-gap theorem is not part of that selected result.

The selected formal statement has narrower scope than the full manuscript claim.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{PrimeGaps.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.Problem344.large\_gaps\_and\_ratio\_density}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

